{
  "result": "The afternoon rain began without ceremony, turning the dusty street into a mirror of grey sky. I sat by the window, listening to water tap against glass like an impatient visitor. There is something humbling about rain: it does not ask permission, it ignores schedules, and yet everything afterward smells greener, as if the world has exhaled.\n\nI thought about how often we confuse stillness with emptiness. A blank page is not empty; it is holding its breath. The moment a pen touches it, the page sighs into story. We are not so different. We collect silences, store them in the small rooms of our chests, and call them ordinary days. But the rain reminds me that ordinary days are merely unnoticed miracles.\n\nThe cat slept on the chair, tail twitching in some dream of chasing. The kettle clicked off in the kitchen. Outside, a child in a yellow raincoat jumped into a puddle and laughed at the small explosion. Joy, I decided, is not a destination you reach after everything is fixed. It is the willingness to jump, to get wet, to accept the messy, temporary splash.\n\nI picked up my notebook and wrote a single sentence, then another. The words were not profound, but they were honest, and honesty is the only soil from which better sentences grow. The rain will stop soon. The street will dry. The memory of the puddle will remain, not because it lasted, but because, for a moment, it held the sky.",
